<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div id="infobox" class="infobox">
  <a id="license-tr" href="https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available">
    License: CC BY 4.0
  </a>
  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2509.19688v1 [cs.RO] 24 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for 
<br class="ltx_break">Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Devesh Nath<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haoran Yin<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Glen Chou
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>The authors are with the Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, USA. <span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{dnath7, hyin95, chou}@gatech.edu</span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a method for formal safety verification of learning-based generative motion planners. Generative motion planners (GMPs) offer advantages over traditional planners, but verifying the safety and dynamic feasibility of their outputs is difficult since neural network verification (NNV) tools scale only to a few hundred neurons, while GMPs often contain millions. To preserve GMP expressiveness while enabling verification, our key insight is to imitate the GMP by stabilizing references sampled from the GMP with a small neural tracking controller and then applying NNV to the closed-loop dynamics. This yields reachable sets that rigorously certify closed-loop safety, while the controller enforces dynamic feasibility. Building on this, we construct a library of verified GMP references and deploy them online in a way that imitates the original GMP distribution whenever it is safe to do so, improving safety without retraining. We evaluate across diverse planners, including diffusion, flow matching, and vision-language models, improving safety in simulation (on ground robots and quadcopters) and on hardware (differential-drive robot).</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motion planning has been transformed by generative models like diffusion and conditional flow matching (CFM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, which learn multimodal trajectory distributions and enable generative motion planners (GMPs) that produce diverse plans from inputs like language or images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
However, ensuring that GMP-generated trajectories satisfy safety and dynamic feasibility is difficult: GMPs often contain millions of parameters, making neural network verification (NNV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> intractable, limiting their use in safety-critical settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. NNV provides hard guarantees via set-based reachability but only scales to controllers with a few hundred neurons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. More scalable statistical methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> yield weaker probabilistic guarantees or require prohibitive samples over long horizons. Thus, existing work trades off between expressive large models lacking hard guarantees and small verifiable models unable to capture complex behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge this gap, we propose <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SaGe-MP</span> (<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Sa</span>fe <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Ge</span>nerative <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">M</span>otion <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">P</span>lanning), a method that provides hard safety and dynamic feasibility guarantees for GMP-generated motion plans. Our key insight is that while NNV tools cannot directly certify the GMP, they can certify a <span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">small</span> neural <span id="S1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tracking controller</span> that locally stabilizes the system around GMP-sampled references.
Reachability analysis of the resulting closed-loop system yields hard assurances of safety and dynamic feasibility over a continuum of inputs. Here, the GMP acts only as an open-loop plan generator, while verification is performed on the closed-loop dynamics induced by tracking a <span id="S1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fixed</span> GMP plan, resulting in a smaller computational graph that makes NNV tractable. By tracking GMP references under the true dynamics, the controller also projects potentially dynamically-infeasible GMP plans onto feasible trajectories. To preserve the original GMP behavior if possible, we develop a trajectory-library approach: multiple GMP references are sampled offline, certified as safely trackable via NNV, and deployed online in a way that mimics the potentially multimodal GMP output. In this sense, our method is a lightweight GMP refinement that enhances safety and dynamic feasibility without costly GMP retraining. Our contributions are:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.19688v1/block_diagram_v3.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/245;" width="476" height="245" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An overview of our method, which samples GMP references and stabilizes them with a small neural tracking controller (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Learned tracking controller design ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>, verifies safety via NNV-based reachability (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Tracking Neural Feedback Loop (T-NFL) ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>-<a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C Safety Verification with Reachability Analysis ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-C</span></a>), and imitates the original GMP with a trajectory library (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS4" title="V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-D</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An NNV-based method for formal safety verification of large GMPs that decouples trajectory generation from neural feedback loop verification, preserving planner expressiveness while providing hard safety guarantees.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A method to certify a neural trajectory tracking controller that stabilizes dynamically-infeasible GMP references, producing safe, feasible trajectories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A trajectory-library method that stores certified-safe GMP plans and executes them online in a way that preserves the original behavior whenever safe. We prove sample complexity bounds for a target imitation error.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive simulation and real-world validation demonstrating safe stabilization of references from diverse generative models (diffusion, flow matching, VLMs, neural ODEs) on challenging nonlinear systems (e.g., 12D quadcopter, and learned NN dynamics).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generative Motion Planners (GMPs)</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GMPs generate diverse, human-like trajectories from rich inputs such as images or language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and can be trained via imitation learning, unlike traditional trajectory optimization or sampling-based planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which are limited to low-dimensional inputs. They also capture multimodal trajectory distributions, representing distinct homotopy classes that serve as a diverse set of solutions to the task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, ensuring safety of GMP-generated trajectories is difficult. Existing methods encourage safety with planning costs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> or use control barrier functions (CBFs) as corrections <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, but often assume simplified dynamics (e.g., single integrator). For nonlinear robot dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, it is difficult to find CBFs; neural CBFs can be learned for such systems, but only heuristically encourage safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. In contrast, we provide hard guarantees for nonlinear NN dynamics by overriding unsafe GMP plans with a certified tracking controller that stabilizes around a nearby safe GMP plan.
Enforcing dynamic feasibility of GMP plans is also difficult. Predicting action sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> ensures feasibility but yields discontinuous outputs that GMPs predict poorly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Some methods enforce feasibility on linear systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Others project GMP references onto the manifold of feasible plans under nonlinear dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> but do not guarantee feasibility and have high replanning cost under model error. Our method instead stabilizes GMP references with a tracking controller, projecting them onto the dynamically-feasible manifold and rejecting errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Verification of NN Controllers</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formal methods.  </span> Reachability analysis verifies that system trajectories remain in a safe set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Set-based methods propagate input and disturbance sets to compute reachable set overapproximations (RSOAs). For NN controllers, abstraction tools like CROWN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> provide affine bounds on outputs and enable RSOA computation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. As the NN size grows, however, the computed RSOAs become highly conservative, revealing a tradeoff between model expressiveness and verification tractability. Thus, NNV-based reachability is limited to small NN controllers (hundreds of neurons) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Symbolic and one-shot reachability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> reduces conservativeness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, but remain limited by NN size. Direct verification of GMPs, with millions of neurons, is intractable. We bridge this gap by stabilizing GMP samples with a small, verified tracking controller, preserving GMP expressiveness while certifying safety over a continuum of initial states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical methods.  </span> Statistical methods that bound RSOA soundness probabilities scale better but give weaker guarantees. Conformal prediction (CP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> ensures reachable set coverage under an exchangeability assumption between the training and test distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, which feedback-controlled, time-varying data often violates. Adaptive CP relaxes this but only offers weaker long-run average coverage assurances. Simulation-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> approximate reachable sets probabilistically, but sample complexity grows quickly with horizon length. In contrast, our method provides hard guarantees robust to distribution shifts, scaling to GMP references hundreds of timesteps long.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider discrete-time nonlinear dynamical systems</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=f(x_{k},u_{k},w_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=f(x_{k},u_{k},w_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the discrete timestep. The terms <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}\in\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}\in\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}\in\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}\in\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}\in\mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}\in\mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the state, control input, and an external disturbance, respectively. We assume that the initial state <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> lies within a known set <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and that the disturbance <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a compact set <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}\doteq\{w\mid\|w\|_{\infty}\leq\overline{w}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}\doteq\{w\mid\|w\|_{\infty}\leq\overline{w}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is known <span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a priori</span>, though we note that these bounds can also be estimated from data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Denote <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{Int}(\underline{a},\overline{a})=\{a\mid\underline{a}\leq a\leq\overline{a}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">Int</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{Int}(\underline{a},\overline{a})=\{a\mid\underline{a}\leq a\leq\overline{a}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, as a hyper-rectangular interval, where <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a},\overline{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>A</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a},\overline{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and the inequalities <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> are interpreted element-wise, i.e., <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a}_{i}\leq a_{i}\leq\overline{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a}_{i}\leq a_{i}\leq\overline{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq A</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-ball as <math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{a}(c)\doteq\{x\mid\|x-c\|_{\infty}\leq a\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{a}(c)\doteq\{x\mid\|x-c\|_{\infty}\leq a\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Computational Graph Robustness Verification</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A computational graph (CG) is a directed acyclic graph representing the sequence of mathematical operations on an input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, such as a neural network or the nonlinear function (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). For a graph <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with input set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and output <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathcal{Z})\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathcal{Z})\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can compute a guaranteed overapproximation of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathcal{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathcal{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> using CROWN-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> tools like <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">auto_LiRPA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. These tools provide guaranteed affine lower and upper bounds, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, on the output <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathcal{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathcal{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> for any hyper-rectangular input set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is formalized in the following proposition from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(CG Robustness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For CG <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and hyper-rectangular <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}\doteq\{z\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{i}}\mid\underline{z}\leq z\leq\overline{z}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>≤</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}\doteq\{z\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{i}}\mid\underline{z}\leq z\leq\overline{z}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are affine functions <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{G}(z)\leq G(z)\leq\overline{G}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{G}(z)\leq G(z)\leq\overline{G}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The inequalities hold element-wise and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{G}(z)=\Psi z+\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{G}(z)=\Psi z+\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G}(z)=\Phi z+\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G}(z)=\Phi z+\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi,\Phi\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{o}\times n_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi,\Phi\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{o}\times n_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reachability Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reachability analysis computes the set of all states that a dynamical system can reach from given initial conditions, under admissible controls and disturbances. For brevity, the policy is written as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}=\pi_{\theta}(x_{k},\eta_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}=\pi_{\theta}(x_{k},\eta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable parameters and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> are external conditioning inputs, e.g., a desired reference trajectory. The closed-loop dynamics are</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(x_{k},\eta_{k}),w_{k})\doteq\tilde{f}(x_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(x_{k},\eta_{k}),w_{k})\doteq\tilde{f}(x_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the dependence on control and noise is implicit in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-B Reachability Analysis ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exact Reachability:</span> We define the exact reachable set of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming the state at time <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the hyper-rectangular set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}\doteq\{x\mid\underline{x}_{k}\leq x\leq\overline{x}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}\doteq\{x\mid\underline{x}_{k}\leq x\leq\overline{x}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle R_{k+1}(\mathcal{X}_{k}\mid\eta_{k})=\{x\mid x=\tilde{f}(x_{k}),\ x_{k}\in\mathcal{X}_{k}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{k+1}(\mathcal{X}_{k}\mid\eta_{k})=\{x\mid x=\tilde{f}(x_{k}),\ x_{k}\in\mathcal{X}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\{x\;\middle|\;\begin{aligned} &amp;x=f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(x_{k},\eta_{k}),w_{k}),\\
&amp;x_{k}\in\mathcal{X}_{k},\ w_{k}\in\mathcal{W}\end{aligned}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\{x\;\middle|\;\begin{aligned} &amp;x=f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(x_{k},\eta_{k}),w_{k}),\\
&amp;x_{k}\in\mathcal{X}_{k},\ w_{k}\in\mathcal{W}\end{aligned}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Since computing exact reachable sets for nonlinear NN-controlled systems is intractable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, we compute reachable set over-approximations (RSOAs) <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}\supseteq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊇</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}\supseteq R</annotation></semantics></math> using NNV tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and applying Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (CG Robustness []). ‣ III-A Computational Graph Robustness Verification ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We denote the certified region of attraction (ROA) as the set of initial states <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}\subseteq\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}\subseteq\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{K}\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{K}\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RSOA Computation:</span> The RSOA can be computed symbolically (i.e., over time horizon <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> at once). The one-step case <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math> is commonly used <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>; however, increasing <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> yields tighter bounds than one-step methods by avoiding per-step overapproximation error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Specifically, let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f^{N}}(\cdot)=\tilde{f}\circ\dotsb\circ\tilde{f}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f^{N}}(\cdot)=\tilde{f}\circ\dotsb\circ\tilde{f}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> compositions of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and let the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote its CG. The <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-step RSOA from timestep <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k+N}^{N}(\mathcal{X}_{k}\mid\eta_{k})=\textsf{Int}\Big(\min_{x\in\mathcal{X}_{k}}\underline{F}^{N}(x),\max_{x\in\mathcal{X}_{k}}\overline{F}^{N}(x)\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">Int</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k+N}^{N}(\mathcal{X}_{k}\mid\eta_{k})=\textsf{Int}\Big(\min_{x\in\mathcal{X}_{k}}\underline{F}^{N}(x),\max_{x\in\mathcal{X}_{k}}\overline{F}^{N}(x)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{F}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{F}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are affine bounding functions for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained using <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">auto_LiRPA</span>. If <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=K</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the full <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-step reachable set from <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, known as one-shot reachability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Partitioning <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> into smaller sub-intervals yields tighter bounds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. While partitioning and symbolic methods yield tighter RSOAs, they are more computationally demanding than one-step methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19688v1/quad12d_plot_1x4.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/121;" width="476" height="121"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">RSOAs computed for a 3D quadcopter (with 12D dynamics), safely tracking a motion plan generated by a diffusion model.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generative Motion Planners</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the following motion planners <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:\mathcal{X}_{I}\times\mathcal{X}_{G}\times\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:\mathcal{X}_{I}\times\mathcal{X}_{G}\times\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that map an initial state <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, goal <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{g}\in\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{g}\in\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and task data <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a natural language command) to generate a reference trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\begin{bmatrix}p_{1},\ldots,p_{K}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\begin{bmatrix}p_{1},\ldots,p_{K}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote learned parameters by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neural ODEs (NODEs)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> learn a neural vector field <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}_{t}=\phi_{\nu}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}_{t}=\phi_{\nu}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> which is numerically integrated from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate a discrete-time trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conditional Flow Matching (CFM)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> learns an ODE <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{z_{t}}=\psi_{\nu}(z_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{z_{t}}=\psi_{\nu}(z_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> transporting a latent sample <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from a prior to a target trajectory distribution, yielding <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by integration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion Models</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> learn a network <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> to reverse a multi-step noising process, iteratively denoising an initial sample <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> in order to generate a clean trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vision-Language Models (VLMs)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> act as zero-shot planners that generate position waypoints from an image and text, which are linearly interpolated to create trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Statement</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are given dynamics (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), disturbance set <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, initial state set <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, goal set <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, a safe set <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> encoding safety and task constraints, and a planner <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:\mathcal{X}_{I}\times\mathcal{X}_{G}\times\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:\mathcal{X}_{I}\times\mathcal{X}_{G}\times\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, which maps an initial state <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, a goal <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\textrm{goal}}\in\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>goal</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\textrm{goal}}\in\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and environment/task data (e.g., images, natural language) to a <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-step reference trajectory <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>. References from <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> may not be dynamically feasible (i.e., consistent with (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)) or safe (i.e., contained in <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>). We assume nothing further about <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>; it may be stochastic and can represent both learned and traditional planners.
We aim to (1) design a controller that replicates the behavior of <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> whenever it is safe to do so and (2) formally verify that the closed-loop system safely reaches <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we aim to solve:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproblem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproblem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 1</span></span><span id="Thmproblem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Design)<span id="Thmproblem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproblem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproblem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproblem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Train an NN control policy <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{I}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{I}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that, given the current state <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and parameters <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, generates closed-loop trajectories from <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> that (1) resemble the open-loop trajectories produced by <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> while (2) ensuring safety and dynamic feasibility.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproblem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproblem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 2</span></span><span id="Thmproblem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Verification)<span id="Thmproblem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproblem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproblem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproblem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Verify that for all <math id="Thmproblem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, the system reaches <math id="Thmproblem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> at timestep <math id="Thmproblem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> under (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-B Reachability Analysis ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) while staying in <math id="Thmproblem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmproblem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Methodology</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We overview our method, SaGe-MP (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), which learns a trajectory tracking controller (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Learned tracking controller design ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>), formulates the closed-loop tracking dynamics for a fixed GMP plan as a CG (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Tracking Neural Feedback Loop (T-NFL) ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>), and performs NN verification on this CG to compute the RSOA and ensure safety (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C Safety Verification with Reachability Analysis ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-C</span></a>). Finally, we sample distinct GMP references to construct a library of certified-safe RSOAs and use it to provide a runtime algorithm to preserve the original GMP behavior (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS4" title="V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learned tracking controller design</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since GMPs typically generate reference trajectories that are not dynamically-feasible, the resulting error must be counteracted, e.g., with replanning or a trajectory-tracking controller. We opt for the latter; in this section, we describe our method for training and formally verifying a tracking controller. Note that we train a <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single</span> small tracking controller that is <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trajectory-conditioned</span>; we do not train a new tracking controller for different references and show that our controller generalizes well to different references in Sec. <a href="#S6" title="VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider tracking controllers <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that map a state <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and reference trajectory <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to a control input. For brevity, at timestep <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we also refer to this controller as <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}=\pi_{\theta}(e_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}=\pi_{\theta}(e_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> has learnable parameters <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> collects reference errors over a future horizon of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> references <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k+1},\ldots,p_{k+N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k+1},\ldots,p_{k+N}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}\doteq[p_{k+1}-x_{k},\ p_{k+2}-x_{k},\ \ldots,\ p_{k+N}-x_{k}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}\doteq[p_{k+1}-x_{k},\ p_{k+2}-x_{k},\ \ldots,\ p_{k+N}-x_{k}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Conditioning on future errors makes <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> focus on reducing tracking error and anticipating changes, specializing to local error correction instead of global planning, which is handled by the expressive GMP.
Thus <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can remain small (<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 100</annotation></semantics></math> neurons), which aids verification by keeping the unrolled CG compact and the resulting RSOA less conservative (cf. baselines in Figs. <a href="#S6.F3.fig1" title="Fig. 3 ‣ VI-A Baseline Comparisons ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S6.F4.fig1" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VI-A Baseline Comparisons ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
We represent <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> as a small multi-layer perceptron (MLP) with leaky-ReLU activations and train it to produce dynamically-consistent corrective actions by minimizing the loss <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{track}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{state}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ctrl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>track</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>state</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ctrl</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{track}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{state}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ctrl}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which combines a mean squared tracking error <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{track}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>track</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{track}}</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth penalties for state <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L}_{\text{state}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>state</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L}_{\text{state}})</annotation></semantics></math> and control <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L}_{\text{ctrl}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ctrl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L}_{\text{ctrl}})</annotation></semantics></math> limit violations over a horizon <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{track}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>track</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{track}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{j=k}^{k+N}\|x_{j+1}-p_{j+1}\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{j=k}^{k+N}\|x_{j+1}-p_{j+1}\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{state}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>state</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{state}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{j=k}^{k+N}\left(\sigma(x_{j+1}-x_{U})+\sigma(x_{L}-x_{j+1})\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{j=k}^{k+N}\left(\sigma(x_{j+1}-x_{U})+\sigma(x_{L}-x_{j+1})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{ctrl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ctrl</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{ctrl}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{j=k}^{k+N}\left(\sigma(u_{j}-u_{U})+\sigma(u_{L}-u_{j})\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{j=k}^{k+N}\left(\sigma(u_{j}-u_{U})+\sigma(u_{L}-u_{j})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the closed-loop state at timestep <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> under controller <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the softplus function, which penalizes violations of hyper-rectangular state and control bounds. To optimize <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute gradients by rolling out <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-step closed-loop trajectories under the dynamics (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), which we assume is differentiable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tracking Neural Feedback Loop (T-NFL)</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the Tracking Neural Feedback Loop (T-NFL), denoted <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, as a CG that models the evolution of the closed-loop tracking dynamics over the execution horizon <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. This graph is constructed by unrolling the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-fold recursive application of the single-step closed-loop dynamics, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(e_{k}),w_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(e_{k}),w_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tracking controller from Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Learned tracking controller design ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-step lookahead reference tracking error.
Specifically, the T-NFL is a function <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> that maps an initial state <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, an <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a priori</span> fixed reference <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from the GMP, and a disturbance sequence <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{K}\}\in\mathcal{W}^{K}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{K}\}\in\mathcal{W}^{K}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times K}</annotation></semantics></math> to the resulting closed-loop trajectory <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})=[\Gamma_{1}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w}),\ldots,\Gamma_{K}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})]\doteq\xi=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{K}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})=[\Gamma_{1}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w}),\ldots,\Gamma_{K}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})]\doteq\xi=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{K}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\Gamma_{k}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})\doteq&amp;\ f(\ldots,f(f(x_{I},\tau_{1:N},w_{1}),\tau_{2:N+1},w_{2}),\\
&amp;\ldots,\tau_{k-1:k+N-2},w_{k}),\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\Gamma_{k}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})\doteq&amp;\ f(\ldots,f(f(x_{I},\tau_{1:N},w_{1}),\tau_{2:N+1},w_{2}),\\
&amp;\ldots,\tau_{k-1:k+N-2},w_{k}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we denote <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}^{K}=\mathcal{W}\times\ldots\times\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}^{K}=\mathcal{W}\times\ldots\times\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-fold Cartesian product of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, at every timestep <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the next <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> references <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k+1},\ldots,p_{k+N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k+1},\ldots,p_{k+N}</annotation></semantics></math> are provided to the tracking controller. To guarantee that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> valid future references are always available, we extend the reference trajectory by padding it with the final point <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> until its length reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+N</annotation></semantics></math>. The process for generating the closed-loop trajectory is detailed in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ V-B Tracking Neural Feedback Loop (T-NFL) ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> T-NFL</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Initial state <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>;
Reference trajectory <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>;
Disturbance sequence <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Closed-loop trajectory rollout <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\leftarrow x_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\leftarrow x_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>,  <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\leftarrow[\;]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\leftarrow[\;]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math>:

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}\leftarrow f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(p_{k+1}-x_{k},\ldots,p_{k+N}-x_{k}),w_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}\leftarrow f(x_{k},\pi_{\theta}(p_{k+1}-x_{k},\ldots,p_{k+N}-x_{k}),w_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\leftarrow[\xi,x_{k+1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\leftarrow[\xi,x_{k+1}]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safety Verification with Reachability Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To formally verify the safety of a generated plan, we compute an RSOA for the full-horizon closed-loop trajectory using one-shot reachability analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. We leverage the T-NFL’s structure as a single computational graph, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents the <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-timestep closed-loop system rollout, from <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,\ldots,K-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,\ldots,K-1</annotation></semantics></math>. This graph is passed to the <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">auto_LiRPA</span> library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, which propagates set-based inputs through the graph to bound the final output set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we encode the uncertain inputs using hyper-rectangular sets. The set of initial states is defined as <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}=\{x\mid\|x-x_{I}\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}=\{x\mid\|x-x_{I}\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the set of possible disturbances at each step is defined as <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\{w\mid\|w\|_{\infty}\leq\overline{w}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\{w\mid\|w\|_{\infty}\leq\overline{w}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, the reference trajectory <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and passed as a deterministic tensor. <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">auto_LiRPA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> passes these inputs through <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to find vector-valued affine bounding functions, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, for the <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-step closed-loop trajectory <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. This enables us to compute the RSOA at each timestep <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by considering specific components of these functions, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Gamma}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Gamma}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Gamma}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Gamma}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which bound the state of the closed-loop system at timestep <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}\in\mathcal{W}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}\in\mathcal{W}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and for all initial conditions <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. This hyper-rectangular set, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, is found by determining the range of these affine bounding functions over the input sets. Since the functions are affine and the input sets are hyper-rectangles, this optimization is tractable and computed efficiently by <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">auto_LiRPA</span>:</p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\hskip-5.0pt\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)\doteq\textsf{Int}\Bigg(\min_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}\\
\mathbf{w}\in\mathcal{W}^{k}\end{subarray}}\underline{\Gamma}_{k}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w}),\max_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}\\
\mathbf{w}\in\mathcal{W}^{k}\end{subarray}}\overline{\Gamma}_{k}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})\Bigg)\hskip-3.0pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≐</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathsize="0.900em">Int</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐰</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐰</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\hskip-5.0pt\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)\doteq\textsf{Int}\Bigg(\min_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}\\
\mathbf{w}\in\mathcal{W}^{k}\end{subarray}}\underline{\Gamma}_{k}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w}),\max_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}\\
\mathbf{w}\in\mathcal{W}^{k}\end{subarray}}\overline{\Gamma}_{k}(x_{I},\tau,\mathbf{w})\Bigg)\hskip-3.0pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We can use these RSOAs <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> to formally verify reach-avoid properties for the closed-loop system. Specifically, we can ensure that the closed-loop system reaches goal set <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> by checking if <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and that the system is safe and satisfies task constraints by checking if <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>. These guarantees hold for all disturbance sequences that may be drawn from <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and all initial conditions drawn from <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety</span></h3>

<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Generation of RSOA library <math id="alg2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            GMP <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>; initial set <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>; goal set <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>;
RSOA budget <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>;
environment <math id="alg2.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>; disturbance set <math id="alg2.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            A set of safe GMP references and RSOAs, <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{C}|&lt;C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{C}|&lt;C</annotation></semantics></math>:

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{c}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{c}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>(<math id="alg2.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if not</span> <span id="alg2.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Collision</span>(<math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau,\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau,\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>):

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, success <math id="alg2.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FindRSOA</span>(<math id="alg2.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I},\tau^{c},\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I},\tau^{c},\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> success <span id="alg2.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">and not</span> <span id="alg2.l8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Collision</span>(<math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>):

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               Add <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau^{c},R^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau^{c},R^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c}))</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key advantage of GMPs is their multimodality: producing diverse, viable trajectories for a task, potentially from different homotopy classes. GMPs are typically trained on multimodal expert demonstration data which encodes desirable behaviors. However, when imitated by the GMP, the resulting plans may not be safe or dynamically feasible. Our goal in Sec. <a href="#S5.SS4" title="V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-D</span></a> is to build on the method of Sec. <a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C Safety Verification with Reachability Analysis ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-C</span></a> to balance two objectives: 1) preserve similarity to the GMP when safe while 2) increasing the safety rate
when starting from the certified ROA <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with our method relative to the executing the raw GMP samples, avoiding costly retraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preserving multimodality.  </span>While following the closed-loop tracking policy defined by a single certified RSOA (and corresponding reference trajectory) ensures robust constraint satisfaction, it also collapses the closed-loop system into a unimodal trajectory distribution, which may not reflect the potential multimodality of the original GMP distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we build upon Sec. <a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C Safety Verification with Reachability Analysis ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-C</span></a> by building a library <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> reference trajectories sampled from the GMP and corresponding safe RSOAs <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}\doteq\{(\tau^{i},\hat{R}_{1:K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{i})\}_{i=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\doteq\{(\tau^{i},\hat{R}_{1:K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{i})\}_{i=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. At runtime, our method chooses a reference from <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to track in order to better imitate the GMP distribution.
Our method for building <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is presented in Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. It repeatedly samples references <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> from the GMP <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and, after an initial constraint check (ensuring that <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{c}_{K}\in\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{c}_{K}\in\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{c}_{k}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{c}_{k}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) and computes the closed-loop RSOA <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{R}_{1:K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{1:K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})</annotation></semantics></math> for tracking <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x,\tau^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x,\tau^{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. If the RSOA satisfies the constraints, i.e., <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{K}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})\subseteq\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}\mid\tau^{c})\subseteq\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, it is added to the library <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; if not, the process repeats until <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> safe RSOAs are found; <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximum RSOA computation budget and can be tuned based on observed imitation error. For efficiency, this process can be parallelized on the GPU across multiple references <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At execution time, a new reference <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math> starting from <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the GMP <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. We select the closest reference trajectory in <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, as measured by the minimum <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> error</p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\tau}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\doteq\min_{i\in\{1,\ldots,C\}}\textstyle\sum_{k=1}^{K}\|\tau_{k}^{i}-\tau_{k}^{\textrm{samp}}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mmultiscripts><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\tau}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\doteq\min_{i\in\{1,\ldots,C\}}\textstyle\sum_{k=1}^{K}\|\tau_{k}^{i}-\tau_{k}^{\textrm{samp}}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where we denote <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\min</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S5.E11" title="In V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Finally, to obtain a safely-stabilizable, dynamically-feasible trajectory, we evaluate the closed-loop dynamics obtained by tracking <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{i^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{i^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the closed-loop trajectory <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi=\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{i^{*}},\mathbf{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi=\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{i^{*}},\mathbf{w})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theoretical Analysis. </span> In the following, for the specific case where the open-loop dynamics (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are deterministic, i.e., <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+1}=f(x_{t},u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+1}=f(x_{t},u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, we derive finite-sample bounds on the rate at which the closed-loop imitation error</p>
<table id="S5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\doteq\|\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{i^{*}})-\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\doteq\|\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{i^{*}})-\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">decreases as the size of the library <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> increases. At a high level, this result shows that if <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be safely stabilized under <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> increases, <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{i^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{i^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math> converges to <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(x_{I}^{\textrm{samp}},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})</annotation></semantics></math> – the trajectory that would have been executed by directly tracking <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, as the library grows, our method becomes less invasive and more closely approximates the GMP trajectory distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdefinition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-safe stabilization)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A reference <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> starting from <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-safely stabilizable if <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{B}_{\mu}(x_{I})\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{B}_{\mu}(x_{I})\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{K}^{K}(\mathcal{B}_{\mu}(x_{I})\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{K}^{K}(\mathcal{B}_{\mu}(x_{I})\mid\tau)\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lipschitz constant of the T-NFL with respect to the reference, i.e., <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Gamma(x_{I},\tau)-\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|\leq L\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Gamma(x_{I},\tau)-\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|\leq L\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. The following result proves a bound on <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that with probability at least <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the error between the original and certified closed-loop trajectories (<a href="#S5.E12" title="In V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\xi^{\textrm{samp}}-\xi^{i^{*}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\xi^{\textrm{samp}}-\xi^{i^{*}}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textrm{Pr}\big[E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\epsilon\big]\geq 1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Pr}\big[E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\epsilon\big]\geq 1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Minimal invasiveness)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that A) the open-loop dynamics (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are deterministic, B) (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> are Lipschitz continuous in all arguments, and C) Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> samples reference trajectories i.i.d. from the GMP <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> be a pre-specified error threshold and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-safely stabilizable reference generated from <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> with the properties that A) for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\epsilon/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\epsilon/L</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Pr}(P\textrm{ draws sample from }\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}}))\doteq p_{\textrm{samp}}\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> draws sample from </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Pr}(P\textrm{ draws sample from }\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}}))\doteq p_{\textrm{samp}}\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and that B) for all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is also <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-safely stabilizable. After <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. draws from <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textrm{Pr}\big[E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\epsilon\big]\geq 1-\exp(-p_{\textrm{samp}}C)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Pr}\big[E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\epsilon\big]\geq 1-\exp(-p_{\textrm{samp}}C),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and to ensure that <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textrm{Pr}\big[E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\epsilon\big]\geq 1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Pr}\big[E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\epsilon\big]\geq 1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, one can select <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\geq\log(1/\alpha)/p_{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">samp</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\geq\log(1/\alpha)/p_{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-safely stabilizable, and the probability of sampling <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math> per draw, the chance of zero successes in <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. trials is <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-(1-p_{\textrm{samp}})^{C}\geq 1-\exp(-p_{\textrm{samp}}C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-(1-p_{\textrm{samp}})^{C}\geq 1-\exp(-p_{\textrm{samp}}C)</annotation></semantics></math>. When some <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{B}_{\delta}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}})</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled, it will be added to <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> as it is <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-safely stabilizable. Then, at runtime, when <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled, from the definition of Lipschitz continuity, we have that <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Gamma(x_{I},\tau)-\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|\leq L\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Gamma(x_{I},\tau)-\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|\leq L\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is guaranteed to exist and be finite since <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> are Lipschitz and <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic. Since <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\epsilon/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\epsilon/L</annotation></semantics></math> and there exists some <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability at least <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\exp(-p_{\textrm{samp}}C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\exp(-p_{\textrm{samp}}C)</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\|\Gamma(x_{I},\tau)-\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|\leq L\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|\leq L\delta\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\xi}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C})\leq\|\Gamma(x_{I},\tau)-\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{\textrm{samp}})\|\leq L\|\tau-\tau^{\textrm{samp}}\|\leq L\delta\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In Sec. <a href="#S6" title="VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, we empirically validate that by collecting even a small number of RSOAs in <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can reduce the trajectory distribution shift with respect to the GMP <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Safe Multimodal Runtime Execution</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            initial state <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>;
GMP reference <math id="alg3.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\textrm{samp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\textrm{samp}}</annotation></semantics></math>;
library <math id="alg3.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>;
tracking controller <math id="alg3.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>;
disturbances <math id="alg3.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Closed-loop trajectory rollout <math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\tau}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C}),i^{*}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mtext>samp</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\tau}(\tau^{\textrm{samp}},\mathcal{C}),i^{*}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Solve (<a href="#S5.E11" title="In V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) 
</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{i}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{i}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Reference <math id="alg3.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg3.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\leftarrow\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{i^{*}},\mathbf{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\leftarrow\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{i^{*}},\mathbf{w})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg3.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verifying large sets of initial conditions.  </span>
We can also extend our approach to verify safe forward invariance for large initial condition sets <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> by blending multiple GMP-generated reference trajectories with the tracking controller <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, naïvely stabilizing a large <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> around a single reference causes the RSOA to grow out of bounds in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent this, we sample multiple reference trajectories <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the GMP, each with different initial conditions <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}^{i}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}^{i}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, and stabilize a smaller neighborhood <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}\doteq\mathcal{B}_{\epsilon}(x_{I}^{i})\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}\doteq\mathcal{B}_{\epsilon}(x_{I}^{i})\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> of initial conditions to <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. This relies on only local trajectory robustness, enabling the tracking controller to remain relatively simple. As in Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we can form a library of references and RSOAs <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}\doteq\{(\tau^{i},\hat{R}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}\mid\tau^{i})\}_{i=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\doteq\{(\tau^{i},\hat{R}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}\mid\tau^{i})\}_{i=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by selecting these initial sets to partition <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}=\bigcup_{i=1}^{C}\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}=\bigcup_{i=1}^{C}\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, at execution time, if <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can use <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and track <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>; the corresponding RSOA certifies that <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> stays in <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we compare to baselines (Sec. <a href="#S6.SS1" title="VI-A Baseline Comparisons ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-A</span></a>), evaluating on nonlinear and learned unicycle and quadcopter dynamics (Sec. <a href="#S6.SS2" title="VI-B Unicycle ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-B</span></a>-<a href="#S6.SS4" title="VI-D 3-D Quadcopter ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-D</span></a>), and demonstrating our method on multimodal GMPs and verification of large <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> sets (Sec. <a href="#S6.SS5" title="VI-E Safe, Multimodal Planning ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-E</span></a>-<a href="#S6.SS6" title="VI-F Verifying forward invariance for large 𝒳_I sets ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-F</span></a>.
were conducted on a desktop computer equipped with an Intel i9-14900K CPU, 64GB of RAM, and an NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU.
For all GMPs other than the pre-trained VLM, we generated training data via trajectory optimization. See App. <a href="#A0.SS1" title="-A Experiment Details ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-A</span></a> for further experiment details.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baseline Comparisons</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We motivate our method by highlighting three challenges in improving the safety of learned planners. First, large NN tracking controllers yield loose RSOAs as computed by NNV tools, motivating smaller controllers. Second, sampling-based verification of RSOAs is highly sample-inefficient over long horizons, motivating NNV tools. Finally, neural CBFs safety filters can be unsound and lead to violations, motivating formally verified controllers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loose RSOAs for large NNs.  </span> We illustrate the tradeoff between NN size and closed-loop RSOA volume. As shown in Fig.<a href="#S6.F3.fig1" title="Fig. 3 ‣ VI-A Baseline Comparisons ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, larger NNs improve expert data recreation error (blue) and shrink the exact reachable set <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I})</annotation></semantics></math> but enlarge the computed RSOA <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}^{K}(\mathcal{X}_{I})</annotation></semantics></math> due to the resulting NNV conservativeness (red). For example, Fig. <a href="#S6.F4.fig1" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VI-A Baseline Comparisons ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows a neural ODE planner with three hidden layers of width 256 yields a conservative RSOA that grows unbounded while the trajectories remain bounded.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;">
<div id="S6.F3.fig1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:189.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19688v1/model_sweep_graph_v5.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:249/181;" width="249" height="181"></object>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S6.F3.fig1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We visualize the trade-off between controller capacity and RSOA volume. While larger models (more neurons) reduce tracking error, they inflate the reachable set volume, leading to more conservative guarantees.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:127.6pt;">
<div id="S6.F4.fig1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:217.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19688v1/node_explosion_and_volume.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:291/157;" width="291" height="157"></object>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.fig1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">RSOA for neural ODE (756 neurons). The rapid RSOA growth shows a looseness issue for NNV with larger models; this is reflected in the RSOA volumes as well (inset).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inefficiency of sampling-based safety analysis.</span> Sampling-based methods are computationally inefficient at uncovering rare but critical failures. In a 2D quadcopter scenario (Fig. <a href="#S6.F5.fig1" title="Fig. 5 ‣ VI-A Baseline Comparisons ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), our NN tracking controller follows a <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100</annotation></semantics></math>-length reference trajectory (black) generated by a neural ODE while avoiding an obstacle (red). After <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> simulations, only seven collisions were found, whereas the computed RSOA intersects the obstacle, immediately certifying the possibility of a violation. The high cost of sampling highlights its limitations and the advantage of reachability-based formal verification.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;">
<div id="S6.F5.fig1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:189.8pt;"><img src="2509.19688v1/2d_learned_frs_failure.png" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:249/182;" width="249" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.fig1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Limitations of sampling-based safety analysis. While <math id="S6.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> random simulations uncovered just seven collisions over a <math id="S6.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100</annotation></semantics></math> horizon, our RSOA-based method can directly certify the potential for collision.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unsafe behavior from learned safety filters.  </span> A baseline neural CBF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> fails to reliably enforce safety. In tracking a neural ODE-generated reference under unicycle dynamics (Fig. <a href="#S6.F6.fig1" title="Fig. 6 ‣ VI-A Baseline Comparisons ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the learned CBF overrides the controller when inputs appear unsafe, yet only 46.4% of 1000 rollouts from <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> remain safe (orange). This is because typically neural CBFs only enforce the CBF conditions on sampled states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, compromising the guarantees. In contrast, our method formally certifies that the closed-loop system is 100% safe.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:127.6pt;">
<div id="S6.F6.fig1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:217.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19688v1/cbf_vs_frs.svg" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:291/114;" width="291" height="114"></object>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.fig1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A neural CBF baseline (pink) frequently fails to ensure safety, while SaGe-MP (blue) guarantees safety.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unicycle</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation results on analytical and learned dynamics.  </span> We evaluate our method on unicycle dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Eq. 13.18]</cite>, discretized with a <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1s</annotation></semantics></math> forward-Euler step, tracking trajectories for <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps. To test applicability to learned models, we train an MLP (3 layers, 64 neurons) on <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> transitions from the ground truth. A NODE planner is trained on ground-truth trajectories, and a tracking controller <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with two hidden layers of width 8 is trained to follow them; the same controller is used in both cases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19688v1/combined_left_right.svg" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/106;" width="476" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 7</span>: </span><span id="S6.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Simulated unicycle RSOAs, with references generated by (A) NODE + analytical dynamics, (B) NODE + learned dynamics.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ VI-B Unicycle ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, under both analytical (a) and learned (b) dynamics, our T-NFL safely tracks neural ODE reference trajectories, producing RSOAs that formally verify safety and goal-reaching. RSOA computation times were <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100.78s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100.78</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100.78s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="51.95s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>51.95</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">51.95s</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. This experiment shows that our method can follow NODE-generated plans and certify closed-loop safety for both analytical and learned dynamics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.19688v1/Real_combined_small_v2.png" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/142;" width="429" height="142" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 8</span>: </span><span id="S6.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Hardware deployment: a plan is generated from an image of the scene and a prompt using a VLM, which is safely executed by our controller on an differential-drive robot.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware results with natural language task constraints.  </span> We demonstrate our framework on a physical wheeled robot (Fig. <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI-B Unicycle ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). A VLM (Gemini 2.5 Pro) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> generated reference trajectories from images and text prompts. We evaluate two examples: one where the VLM generates a collision-free path to the goal (Fig. <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI-B Unicycle ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>a), and another where it reaches the target while moving over a food item and treating other objects as obstacles (Fig. <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI-B Unicycle ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>b). See App. <a href="#A0.SS1" title="-A Experiment Details ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-A</span></a> for prompt details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The VLM produces reference trajectories as discussed in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Generative Motion Planners ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>. The same tracking controller from the simulation experiments is used to follow these trajectories. At runtime on hardware, the vehicle stayed within the computed RSOA (Fig. <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI-B Unicycle ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). Despite being trained only on NODE-generated references, the controller successfully tracked VLM-generated trajectories, with formal verification providing guaranteed RSOA computation even out-of-distribution. These results show that our controller generalizes to unseen reference trajectory distributions and safely stabilizes plans from images and language, ensuring task completion from any state in <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Planar Quadcopter</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we evaluate our framework on a planar, 6D quadcopter model (App. <a href="#A0.SS1" title="-A Experiment Details ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-A</span></a>), time-discretized with a timestep of <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>s for a horizon of <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100</annotation></semantics></math>. The task is to navigate a <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 5\;m^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 5\;m^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> workspace containing a circular obstacle.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19688v1/2d_quad_combined_plot_2x2.svg" id="S6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/196;" width="429" height="196"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 9</span>: </span><span id="S6.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">RSOAs computed for the planar quadcopter for various planners, verifying safety: (A) diffusion + analytical dynamics (AD); (B) CFM + AD; (C) NODE + learned dynamics; (D) NODE + AD.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analytical dynamics.  </span>As shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ VI-C Planar Quadcopter ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, our framework certifies plans from diverse learned planners, including diffusion, CFM, and neural ODE models. The tracking controller (2 hidden layers, 8 neurons each) reliably follows the generated trajectories, yielding collision-free closed-loop RSOAs and certifying safety for all <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. RSOA computation times are comparable to before, averaging <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="123.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>123.21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">123.21</annotation></semantics></math>s for analytical dynamics and <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>59.85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.85</annotation></semantics></math>s for the learned model, using 4 and 25 initial partitions, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a surrogate for measuring the increased safety rate when starting from the certified ROA with our method relative to the GMP, we consider a fixed input set <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and compute empirical safety rates for 1) open-loop and 2) closed-loop execution of GMP plans. Out of 126 references sampled from the diffusion planner, 1) 78.5% and 2) 94.4% were safe starting from <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the same input set, we also evaluated the CFM-based planner, which yielded 68.5% and 81.5% safety out of 108 samples. In contrast, we are able to certify 100% safety; i.e., <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for diffusion and CFM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, this experiment suggests that SaGe-MP stabilizes noisy, dynamically-infeasible references from diffusion and CFM (Fig. <a href="#S6.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ VI-C Planar Quadcopter ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, purple), increasing the safety rate by certifying the ROA and effectively “projecting” them onto the set of feasible trajectories that safely reach the goal, improving on the approximate projection of  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learned dynamics.  </span>To illustrate scalability to higher-dimensional learned dynamics, we trained an NN dynamics model (3 hidden layers, 64 neurons each) from transitions of the analytical model, along with a tracking controller (2 layers, 16 neurons each). The resulting closed-loop RSOAs again show safe tracking (Fig. <a href="#S6.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ VI-C Planar Quadcopter ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>c). These results highlight the planner-agnostic nature of our method and ability to safely track trajectories from GMPs on underactuated dynamics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span> </span><span id="S6.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3-D Quadcopter</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test scalability on an analytical 12-state 3D quadcopter model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, discretized with a <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>s timestep over a <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100</annotation></semantics></math> horizon. The task is set in a <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 5\times 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 5\times 5</annotation></semantics></math> m<sup id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup> workspace with a spherical obstacle of radius <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>m. We train a tracking controller <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, with 2 hidden layers of 8 neurons each, to track a diffusion-generated reference. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Reachability Analysis ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the computed RSOA guarantees safety: the controller tracks the reference trajectory while the certified RSOA remains collision-free, computed in <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2153.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2153.45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2153.45</annotation></semantics></math>s with 4 uniform partitions of <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This demonstrates that our method can scale to certifiably stabilize GMP-generated references on high-dimensional, underactuated nonlinear systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">VI-E</span> </span><span id="S6.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe, Multimodal Planning</span></h3>

<figure id="S6.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19688v1/combined_2x2.svg" id="S6.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/309;" width="476" height="309"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 10</span>: </span><span id="S6.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We find one safe RSOA via Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for diffusion (A) and CFM (C) planners. We illustrate our trajectory library approach (B), which can capture multiple modes of the GMP and reduce the imitation error relative to the original GMP (D).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate that generating multiple plans from <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the multimodality of the GMP. For analytical planar quadcopter dynamics (Fig. <a href="#S6.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ VI-E Safe, Multimodal Planning ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>c), at a deliberately selected <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, the diffusion-based planner produces two modes - above and below the obstacle. We sample five safe references from the GMP and compute their RSOAs, visualizing two in Fig. <a href="#S6.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ VI-E Safe, Multimodal Planning ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>c. At runtime, an initial state <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}\in\bigcup_{i=1}^{C}\mathcal{B}_{\epsilon}(x_{I}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}\in\bigcup_{i=1}^{C}\mathcal{B}_{\epsilon}(x_{I}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is measured, and the GMP is queried to return a reference trajectory that starts from <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. We follow Alg. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, showing that tracking the selected reference with <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can well-imitate the GMP trajectory distribution even with <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the imitation error (<a href="#S5.E12" title="In V-D RSOA libraries for multimodality and improved safety ‣ V Methodology ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) decreases (Fig. <a href="#S6.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ VI-E Safe, Multimodal Planning ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>d); the raw error between the original GMP reference <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and our closed-loop rollout <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{i^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(x_{I},\tau^{i^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math> also decreases. Similarly, with analytical unicycle dynamics and a VLM-based GMP, our method certifies safe RSOAs around several sampled references; three are shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F11" title="Fig. 11 ‣ VI-E Safe, Multimodal Planning ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>a. Moreover, as a surrogate for measuring the increase in safety rate when starting from the certified ROA <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the GMP, we are able to certify 100% safety, i.e., <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}\subseteq\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, while naïvely sampling and stabilizing plans with <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> from the VLM leads to only 16% safety over 50 trials, shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F11" title="Fig. 11 ‣ VI-E Safe, Multimodal Planning ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>b. These results suggest our method can capture multimodal GMP behavior while improving safety rates relative to the original GMP. Moreover, this shows that the invasiveness of our approach decreases as <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> increases, with small error even for small <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.19688v1/vlm_robust_combined.png" id="S6.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/162;" width="476" height="162" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 11</span>: </span><span id="S6.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(A) We illustrate our trajectory-library sampling approach on a VLM planner. VLM references that are unsafe (red) or have RSOAs in collision (purple) are discarded until a set of certified-safe RSOAs and references are collected (green). (B) Naïvely executing the VLM as a planner leads to only 16% safety starting from the green <math id="S6.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas with our method enables 100% safety.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">VI-F</span> </span><span id="S6.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Verifying forward invariance for large <math id="S6.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> sets</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate blending GMP samples with the tracking controller <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to verify safe goal reachability for large initial sets <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. We partition <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> into a grid, sample a NODE-based reference from each cell center, and compute the corresponding RSOA. Fig. <a href="#S6.F12.fig1" title="Fig. 12 ‣ VI-F Verifying forward invariance for large 𝒳_I sets ‣ VI Results ‣ Formal Safety Verification and Refinement for Generative Motion Planners via Certified Local Stabilization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows certified collision-free RSOAs (green) and those in collision (red). Once computed offline, these RSOAs allow fast evaluation of which subsets of <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> remain safe under new obstacle configurations, via collision-checking between hyper-rectangular RSOAs and the environment, taking <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.122\pm 0.003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.122</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.003</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.122\pm 0.003</annotation></semantics></math>s seconds. While grid-based partitioning works best in low dimensions, this experiment shows pre-certification enables efficient real-time safety queries; moreover, to reduce partitioning in higher dimensions, one can employ adaptive gridding.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F12.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;">
<div id="S6.F12.fig1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;"><img src="2509.19688v1/frs_safe_funnel_plot.png" id="S6.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:224/184;" width="224" height="184" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F12.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 12</span>: </span><span id="S6.F12.fig1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We verify forward invariance for a large set of initial conditions (green) by blending our tracking controller with several sampled references from a NODE-based planner, on analytical planar quadcopter dynamics.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present SaGe-MP, a method for formally verifying the safety and dynamic feasibility of motion plans from large GMPs. Our approach stabilizes GMP-sampled references with a small neural tracking controller and applies NNV to the resulting closed-loop dynamics, enabling rigorous reachability guarantees even when the GMP is too large for direct analysis. By certifying multiple references offline and deploying them online via a trajectory library, our method preserves the GMP’s multimodal behavior while increasing safety rate when starting from a certified-safe region of attraction.
In future work, we will explore tighter NN relaxations, e.g., those based on branch-and-bound, to reduce the conservativeness of our approach and enable faster reachable set calculations to enable safe deployment in nonstationary environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
